# Line endings are LF everywhere: in the repository and in every working tree.
#
# Without this file, `core.autocrlf` decides — a per-user setting the repository
# cannot see and does not control. That produced a working tree where line
# endings varied file by file depending on whether git had checked a file out or
# a tool had rewritten it, which broke three things at once:
#
#   * `gofmt -l` reported 188 of 248 Go files as unformatted, all of it line-ending
#     noise, which is why formatting was never enforced;
#   * tools parsing repository text saw a trailing \r inside the last token, which
#     pflag reports as an unknown flag with nothing visible in the message;
#   * docker/harness/start-bitbucket.sh, which the Dockerfile COPYs into a Linux
#     image, acquired a CRLF shebang and failed with `$'\r': command not found`.
#
# eol=lf rather than plain `text=auto` is deliberate. `text=auto` alone
# normalises the repository but still checks out CRLF on Windows, which leaves
# every problem above in place locally. Go, Git Bash, PowerShell 7 and VS Code
# all handle LF on Windows; the shell scripts and container files genuinely
# cannot handle CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files must never be touched. Marking them explicitly is cheaper than
# trusting git's heuristics on a file it has not seen before.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.tgz binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.exe binary
